f43"},"scope":"Methodological findings of CL 8H and GT E5A; no shared formal object is asserted.","to":"GT::GT-F82-E5A-COMPARATOR-AND-NUMERICAL-QUALIFICATIONS"}
{"alternatives":["A unified statement may exist: every clock is a random time change, and CL adds a projection"],"comparison":{"clock":{"note":"CL: epoch subsequence of pair events vs full events; GT: continuous time vs embedded jump chain","verdict":"differs"},"conditioning":{"note":"stationary","verdict":"same"},"horizon":{"note":"CL: next-event statistics; GT: relaxation times","verdict":"differs"},"intervention":{"note":"none","verdict":"not_applicable"},"projection":{"note":"CL epoch also projects out triad events; GT has no projection between its clocks","verdict":"differs"},"state":{"note":"CL: N=3 pair+triad; GT: N=5 graphs","verdict":"differs"},"support":{"note":"positive rates","verdict":"same"},"transition_law":{"note":"different rule families","verdict":"differs"}},"connection_id":"X::0003","connection_type":"explicit_contrast","controls":{"negative":["GT clocks share the state sequence by construction"],"positive":["CL-T15 relates the epoch law to the full-event law by a resolvent"]},"counterevidence_refs":[],"decision_category":"accepted_bounded","decision_history":[{"actor":"agent: Claude Opus 5.5 (session of 2026-10-15)","date":"2026-10-15","decision_category":"accepted_bounded","human":false,"rationale":"Agent review on 2026-10-15: The contrast of clock operations follows from the two definitions. Awaiting the author's acceptance.","status":"agent_reviewed"},{"actor":"author (repository owner): implicit acceptance through the round trip","basis":"implicit_round_trip_acceptance","date":"2026-10-15","decision_category":"accepted_bounded","decision_id":"AD-0002","human":true,"rationale":"Accepted implicitly through round trip RT-0001 (AD-0002) at the reported decision category.","round_trip":"RT-0001","status":"human_accepted"}],"epistemic_basis":"formal","evidence_refs":["CL::CL-T15","GT::GT-F69-CLOCK-SEPARATED-SORTING"],"falsifiers":["A GT case where switching between continuous and jump clocks alone changes Markov order (impossible under the embedded-chain construction)"],"from":"CL::F003-CLOCK-SPLIT","independence":"shared_lineage","independence_note":"Shared lineage of the DSL; the contrast is between formal operations, not between computed numbers.","origin":"agent_drafted","rationale":"Both programmes find that conclusions depend on the clock, but the operations are not the same. CL's pair-event epoch counts only pair events and hides triad events, so a first-order full process looks memoryful on the epoch (projection plus time change). GT's continuous time and jump clock share one event sequence and differ only in holding-time weighting, so they change orderings (relaxation, sorting) but cannot create apparent memory. Treating them as one phenomenon would be an error; the shared theme is that the clock belongs to the estimand.","review":{"date":"2026-10-15","decision_id":"AD-0002","human":true,"record":"proposals/reviews/AD-0002.md","reviewer":"author (repository owner): implicit acceptance through the round trip"},"review_status":"human_accepted","review_vector":{"counterexamples_or_nulls":{"assessment":"A unified statement may exist: every clock is a random time change, and CL adds a projection"},"evidence_status":{"assessment":"The contrast of clock operations follows from the two definitions","status":"mixed"},"falsifiability":{"assessment":"A GT case where switching between continuous and jump clocks alone changes Markov order (impossible under the embedded-chain construction)"},"formal_comparability":{"assessment":"state: differs; transition_law: differs; support: same"},"independence":{"assessment":"shared_lineage: Shared lineage of the DSL; the contrast is between formal operations, not between computed numbers."},"observable_comparability":{"assessment":"projection: differs; clock: differs"},"research_relevance":{"assessment":"CL Generation 3C (N=3 independent triad) and GT E0A (FREE N=5). Both report clock dependence; the clock operations differ."},"scope_compatibility":{"assessment":"horizon: differs; conditioning: same; intervention: not_applicable"},"semantic_resemblance":{"assessment":"lexical retrieval only; not evidence","method":"TF-IDF cosine over statements (rssynth discover, 2026-10-15); a ranking aid only","retrieval_score":0.023507}},"reviewed_source_commits":{"CL":"e650d71bda7203c8018d8bd134c5496a03d74a11","GT":"79fa6b0119449bd56359acf4436015df27855f43"},"scope":"CL Generation 3C (N=3 independent triad) and GT E0A (FREE N=5). Both report clock dependence; the clock operations differ.","to":"GT::GT-F20-TRIAD-CLOCK-REVERSAL"}
{"alternatives":["Some CL Generation 1 cells may be label-covariant by accident; those should then be reversible"],"comparison":{"clock":{"note":"stationary law (clock-free statement)","verdict":"same"},"conditioning":{"note":"none","verdict":"same"},"horizon":{"note":"stationary","verdict":"same"},"intervention":{"note":"none","verdict":"not_applicable"},"projection":{"note":"full state","verdict":"not_applicable"},"state":{"note":"CL N=2-5 labelled graphs; GT N=5,6","verdict":"differs"},"support":{"note":"positive within the declared K bounds","verdict":"same"},"transition_law":{"note":"labelled literal rules vs covariant witness-count templates","verdict":"differs"}},"connection_id":"X::0004","connection_type":"explicit_contrast","controls":{"negative":["GT-F13: covariant two-witness PATH templates are irreversible"],"positive":["GT: 32 gradient cells reversible to 6.5e-14"]},"counterevidence_refs":[],"decision_category":"accepted_bounded","decision_history":[{"actor":"agent: Claude Opus 5.5 (session of 2026-10-15)","date":"2026-10-15","decision_category":"accepted_bounded","human":false,"rationale":"Agent review on 2026-10-15: The rule classes are read from both programmes' definitions. Awaiting the author's acceptance.","status":"agent_reviewed"},{"actor":"author (repository owner): implicit acceptance through the round trip","basis":"implicit_round_trip_acceptance","date":"2026-10-15","decision_category":"accepted_bounded","decision_id":"AD-0002","human":true,"rationale":"Accepted implicitly through round trip RT-0001 (AD-0002) at the reported decision category.","round_trip":"RT-0001","status":"human_accepted"}],"epistemic_basis":"formal","evidence_refs":["GT::GT-F13-NONGRADIENT-ARROW"],"falsifiers":["A label-covariant single-witness GT template that is irreversible","An irreversible CL Generation 1 cell whose rule set is label-covariant and single-witness"],"from":"CL::F001-PAIRWISE-RISE","independence":"shared_lineage","independence_note":"Same DSL lineage; the contrast is decided by rule class, read from both programmes' definitions.","origin":"agent_drafted","rationale":"An apparent contradiction ('reweighting can break reversibility' versus 'every single-witness composition is reversible') dissolves on the formal objects. CL Generation 1 rules are labelled: each acts on one named edge and reads present/absent literals on other named edges (constraint-lab/docs/constraint-dsl.md). GT-F12 covers only label-covariant templates whose weight depends on witness counts. Label covariance plus single-witness structure gives a Gibbs potential; labelled multi-literal rules need not. GT-F13 shows label-covariant two-witness templates also break reversibility. The boundary is label covariance and witness arity.","review":{"date":"2026-10-15","decision_id":"AD-0002","human":true,"record":"proposals/reviews/AD-0002.md","reviewer":"author (repository owner): implicit acceptance through the round trip"},"review_status":"human_accepted","review_vector":{"counterexamples_or_nulls":{"assessment":"Some CL Generation 1 cells may be label-covariant by accident; those should then be reversible"},"evidence_status":{"assessment":"The rule classes are read from both programmes' definitions","status":"mixed"},"falsifiability":{"assessment":"A label-covariant single-witness GT template that is irreversible; An irreversible CL Generation 1 cell whose rule set is label-covariant and single-witness"},"formal_comparability":{"assessment":"state: differs; transition_law: differs; support: same"},"independence":{"assessment":"shared_lineage: Same DSL lineage; the contrast is decided by rule class, read from both programmes' definitions."},"observable_comparability":{"assessment":"projection: not_applicable; clock: same"},"research_relevance":{"assessment":"Reversibility of memoryless pairwise reweighting: CL Generation 1 labelled rules (N=2-5) versus GT label-covariant single-witness templates (O=2, N=5,6)."},"scope_compatibility":{"assessment":"hori